      "content": "Hey, i'm working on an embedded C++ project for an automotive ECU and i've run into a really annoying issue. The system has a gradual RAM exhaustion that only shows up after about 4-5 hours of continuous operation. I've checked the main loops but everything looks okay. Here is a snippet of the telemetry processing class where i suspect something might be going on:\n\n```cpp\nclass TelemetryProcessor {\nprivate:\n    std::vector<uint8_t>* buffer;\n    size_t bufferSize;\n\npublic:\n    TelemetryProcessor(size_t initialSize) : bufferSize(initialSize) {\n        buffer = new uint8_t[bufferSize];\n    }\n\n    void processPacket(const uint8_t* data, size_t len) {\n        if (len > bufferSize) {\n            // Attempting to resize if packet is too large\n            delete[] buffer;\n            bufferSize += len; \n            buffer = new uint8_t[bufferSize];\n        }\n        std::memcpy(buffer, data, len);\n    }\n\n    ~TelemetryProcessor() {\n        delete[] buffer;\n    }\n};\n```\n\nCan you give me a systematic way to track down where exactly the leak is happening? It's hard to catch with standard tools because of the real-time constraints on the hardware.",
      "source": "evaluator"
