      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The logging approach can be a bit tricky in real-time systems, where every cycle counts. In that case, we'll have to rely on more subtle techniques to track down the leak.\n\nNow, regarding the `processPacket` function, I notice that you're using `delete[] buffer` before allocating a new buffer. This is where the issue lies. When you delete an array of objects using `delete[]`, the memory is not immediately returned to the heap. Instead, it's marked as free, and the memory is only actually released when the program terminates.\n\nIn your case, the `buffer` is being deleted, but the memory is not being reused immediately. The new buffer is being allocated, but the old memory is not being freed. This is why the memory leak persists.\n\nThink of it like a goblin's lair. The goblin has taken over the old lair, but it hasn't vacated it yet. The new lair is being built, but the old one is still occupied, causing the memory to leak.\n\nTo fix this issue, you should use `delete[] buffer` only after you've finished using the new buffer. You can do this by moving the `delete[] buffer` statement to the end",
